From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] stream_lavf: keep the HTTP connection across seeks

Every seek of an http(s) stream opened a connection: TCP, TLS, then the
request. With FFmpeg patches 0080 and 0081 the protocol can keep one, and
this sets the two options that make it do so where it is cheap, for
http:// and https:// URLs: multiple_requests=1 (ask for keep-alive) and
short_seek_size=262144. A reply that was read to its end, which is what
reading the index at the end of a Matroska or MP4 file leaves behind,
hands its connection to the request that follows; so does one with at
most 256 KiB left, which are read and dropped. Any other seek opens a
connection as before. short_seek_size is set because its default is the
socket's receive buffer: megabytes on Linux and Android once it has
grown, nothing on Windows.

The options are set in the stream layer's own open, not in
mp_setup_av_network_options(): they do not reach the streams a
libavformat demuxer opens (HLS segments), and --stream-lavf-o overrides
them (multiple_requests=0 restores a connection per seek).

initial_request_size is left to the embedder. It bounds the first
request, so that the seek from the headers to the index can keep the
connection too, and a resumed Matroska file opens on one. But a file
that needs more than that many bytes before its first frame pays one
more round trip (at 512 KiB: a 10 Mbit/s ffmpeg-muxed Matroska file, any
40 Mbit/s file, an MP4 with a moov larger than it), so it has to be
chosen per file; DOCS/man/options.rst says how.

Measured as for patch 0240, with that patch applied, https, median
(range) of 3 runs, connections in brackets; the options were passed with
--stream-lavf-o on the same build:

  RTT 40 ms   resume 30 min      509 (506-524) [3] -> 416 (412-422) [2]
              first seek         487 (470-506) [2] -> 449 (401-477) [1]
              ffmpeg-muxed file,
              resume 30 min      510 (497-513) [3] -> 424 (403-429) [2]
  RTT 100 ms  resume 30 min      1093 (1058-1099) [3] -> 849 (834-852) [2]
              first seek         920 (901-927) [2] -> 765 (728-782) [1]

With initial_request_size=524288 the mkvmerge resume is one connection:
338 (326-339) ms at 40 ms, 688 (683-705) at 100 ms. An open at 0 does
not change (180 -> 177 ms at 40 ms).

diff --git a/DOCS/man/options.rst b/DOCS/man/options.rst
index 817290d..48c2010 100644
--- a/DOCS/man/options.rst
+++ b/DOCS/man/options.rst
@@ -8105,6 +8105,24 @@ Miscellaneous
     other options such as e.g. user agent are not available with all protocols,
     and printing errors for unknown options would end up being too noisy.)
 
+    For ``http://`` and ``https://`` URLs, mpv itself sets ``reconnect=1``,
+    ``reconnect_delay_max=7``, and, to keep the connection across a seek that
+    leaves a reply at or near its end (such as the one that read a file's
+    index), ``multiple_requests=1`` and ``short_seek_size=262144``. Values
+    given here override them; ``multiple_requests=0`` restores a new
+    connection for every seek.
+
+    ``initial_request_size=<bytes>`` additionally bounds the first request of
+    a file, so that leaving it for an index at the end of the file leaves at
+    most that much to read, and the connection can be kept if no more than
+    ``short_seek_size`` of it is left. It pays off when playback resumes at a
+    saved position in a file whose headers and first two video frames (roughly
+    a quarter second of its bitrate) fit in it; reading on past it costs one
+    more round trip.
+
+    Unlike mpv's own values, options given here also reach the streams a
+    libavformat demuxer opens itself, such as HLS segments.
+
     This is a key/value list option. See `List Options`_ for details.
 
 ``--backdrop-type=<auto|none|mica|acrylic|mica-alt>``
diff --git a/stream/stream_lavf.c b/stream/stream_lavf.c
index 7910b82..3e3c102 100644
--- a/stream/stream_lavf.c
+++ b/stream/stream_lavf.c
@@ -404,6 +404,18 @@ static int open_f(stream_t *stream)
     av_dict_set(&dict, "reconnect", "1", 0);
     av_dict_set(&dict, "reconnect_delay_max", "7", 0);
 
+    // Keep the connection across seeks where that is cheap: a reply that
+    // was read to its end (the index at the end of a file) hands its
+    // connection to the next request, and so does one with at most
+    // short_seek_size bytes left, which are read and dropped. Every other
+    // seek opens a connection as before. --stream-lavf-o overrides both, and
+    // initial_request_size there extends this to the first request of a file.
+    bstr proto = mp_split_proto(bstr0(filename), NULL);
+    if (bstr_equals0(proto, "http") || bstr_equals0(proto, "https")) {
+        av_dict_set(&dict, "multiple_requests", "1", 0);
+        av_dict_set(&dict, "short_seek_size", "262144", 0);
+    }
+
     mp_setup_av_network_options(&dict, NULL, stream->global, stream->log);
 
     AVIOInterruptCB cb = {
